WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.560
A growth factor floats outside the cell and never crosses the membrane.

2
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.880
Only one narrow helix connects outside to inside.

3
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.940
So how does a signal outside switch on an enzyme on the far side?

4
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:22.440
For receptor tyrosine kinases, the answer begins by bringing two receptors together.

5
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:27.653
Every receptor tyrosine kinase shares one three-part plan.

6
00:00:28.933 --> 00:00:31.733
Outside sits the ectodomain that catches the ligand.

7
00:00:32.913 --> 00:00:34.553
One helix spans the membrane.

8
00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:41.233
Inside hangs a kinase with a small upper lobe and a large lower lobe, plus a tail

9
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:42.353
carrying tyrosines.

10
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:46.193
Left alone, that kinase holds itself shut.

11
00:00:47.473 --> 00:00:52.793
In the insulin receptor, a flexible stretch called the activation loop plugs its own

12
00:00:52.793 --> 00:00:53.793
active site.

13
00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:59.347
To release that brake, cells bring two kinases together in at least three ways.

14
00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:04.987
Some ligands, like nerve growth factor, are built as pairs that bridge two

15
00:01:04.987 --> 00:01:12.227
receptors. Epidermal growth factor binds one receptor and opens a hidden arm, so two

16
00:01:12.227 --> 00:01:13.887
receptor arms clasp together.

17
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.210
The insulin receptor works differently.

18
00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:23.730
Its two halves are already joined by disulfide bonds, with their kinase legs held

19
00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:29.310
apart at rest. Binding insulin shifts the whole shape, pulling those inner legs

20
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:35.530
together. All three routes end in the same place: two resting kinases side by side.

21
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:39.533
Now the paired kinases release each other's brake.

22
00:01:40.793 --> 00:01:45.713
In the insulin receptor and most others, each kinase adds phosphates to the

23
00:01:45.713 --> 00:01:47.333
activation loop of its partner.

24
00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:51.613
Those phosphates swing the blocking loop clear of the cleft.

25
00:01:52.753 --> 00:01:56.213
The two lobes close on ATP, and catalysis begins.

26
00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:00.987
The epidermal growth factor receptor breaks that pattern.

27
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:04.807
It needs no phosphate on its activation loop.

28
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:08.847
Instead, the two kinases form a lopsided pair.

29
00:02:10.047 --> 00:02:14.587
The large lobe of one kinase presses against the small lobe of its neighbor.

30
00:02:15.847 --> 00:02:19.067
That push opens only the pressed copy into its active shape.

31
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:21.990
Notice that division of labor.

32
00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:26.030
The pressing copy only needs to supply a surface.

33
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:28.590
Now pause and predict.

34
00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.390
HER2 and HER3 are relatives of the epidermal growth factor receptor.

35
00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:39.170
HER2 has a working kinase; HER3 barely has one.

36
00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.910
If HER2 pairs with HER3, can that mixed pair still switch on?

37
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.090
Yes, the mixed pair signals strongly.

38
00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:58.290
Nearly inactive HER3 supplies the pressing large-lobe surface, switching HER2 on.

39
00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:04.190
Whether through loop phosphorylation or lobe-to-lobe contact, the core rule holds.

40
00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:10.830
Bringing two receptor kinases together releases self-inhibition and activates the

41
00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:11.830
pair.

42
00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:15.513
One lonely helix cannot twist a resting kinase awake.

43
00:03:16.773 --> 00:03:21.493
A ligand may bridge two chains, expose a hidden arm, or shift a pre-built pair.

44
00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:24.853
Either way, the payoff stays the same.

45
00:03:26.173 --> 00:03:29.513
Pulling two receptor kinases together opens the switch.
